<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Austria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> is a historical land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a> river, with Linz as its capital, and is now one of Austria's federal states. Known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> as the land above the Enns, it gradually became a separate land from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>. Its towns include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steyr">Steyr</a>, seat of the Otakar margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, and Enns. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innviertel">Innviertel</a> was added from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1779.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-austria.jpg" alt="Upper Austria" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nightstallion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tirol_and_Upper_Austria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> is one of the nine states of Austria, with its capital at Linz. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and the Austrian states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, and has an area of 11,982 square kilometres. Historically it was divided into four regions: the Hausruckviertel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innviertel">Innviertel</a>, Mühlviertel and Traunviertel. Under Roman rule the parts south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> belonged to the province of Noricum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> expanded as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a>, while the lands further west belonged to the Old Bavarians. Bavaria came under Carolingian rule by 788, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 the region belonged to the East Frankish Realm, though Hungarian raids devastated it in the first half of the tenth century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955 it was consolidated within the Duchy of Bavaria.</p><p>In 1156 the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> became a duchy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">House of Babenberg</a>, and by the mid-thirteenth century the region was known as the Principality above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a>, a name first recorded in 1264. By the end of the century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> ruled the duchy, which became an archduchy in 1453.</p><p>In 1490 the area above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a>, centred on Linz, received a measure of provincial autonomy. By 1550 it had a Protestant majority, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> was introduced under Emperor Rudolf II and his successor Matthias. Bavaria controlled the area for some years in the early seventeenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innviertel">Innviertel</a> was ceded from Bavaria in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> in 1779. French armies occupied the land more than once in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p><p>The two archduchies, above and below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a>, kept separate administrations through the nineteenth century, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> collapsed in 1918 the name Upper Austria was officially adopted. After Austria was annexed by Nazi Germany it formed part of Reichsgau Oberdonau, and in 1945 it was divided between an American zone to the south and a Soviet zone to the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> has about 1.5 million inhabitants and is Austria's leading industrial region. It is divided into 15 districts, the three statutory cities of Linz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steyr">Steyr</a> and Wels, and 438 municipalities. Its state constitution defines it as an independent state of the Republic of Austria. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it received a million refugees.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upper Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is named after a cemetery above the salt mines of Hallstatt in <b>Upper Austria</b>, where Johann Georg Ramsauer began digging in 1846 and more than a thousand graves have since been found; the salt in the mines preserved textiles, wooden tools, shoes and leather backpacks.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1804 Francis II proclaimed the Austrian Empire, in which the archduchy remained a crown land until 1918; its territory now forms the federal states of Lower Austria, <b>Upper Austria</b> and Vienna.</blockquote><a href="/state/archduchy-of-austria">Archduchy of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1056 it was ruled by the Otakar family of Steyr, and the territory took its name, Styria (Steiermark), from their castle of Steyr in <b>Upper Austria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-styria">Margraviate of Styria</a></li></ul></section>
